    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Sync job applicants from Google Sheets to Notion and email them by stage\n\n**Who's it for**\nSmall recruiting teams and hiring managers who collect applications through a Google Form and track\ncandidates in a Notion database, and who want new applicants added automatically and stage emails\nsent without anyone copying rows between tools.\n\n**How it works**\nTwo independent flows share one Notion database. The intake flow runs on a schedule: it reads new\napplicants from the Google Sheet behind your form, reads the candidates already in Notion, and drops\nanyone who is already there — so re-running never creates a duplicate page. Genuinely new people are\nadded to Notion and the sheet row is marked processed, which is what stops the next run picking it\nup again. The email flow is triggered by Notion itself: when a candidate's stage changes, the\nprevious stage is read from a data table and compared with the new one, the matching email template\nis fetched from Notion, filled in with that candidate's details, and sent through Gmail. Storing the\nprevious stage is what makes the trigger reliable — Notion reports that a page changed, not what\nchanged about it.\n\n**How to set up**\n1. Create a Notion database for candidates with a stage property, and a second one holding one email\n   template per stage.\n2. Attach Notion, Google Sheets and Gmail credentials to the credential nodes.\n3. Point **`[cred] Sheets - read new applicants`** at the response sheet behind your Google Form.\n4. Set the stage names in **`route by candidate stage`** to match your own.\n\n**Requirements**\nA Notion workspace, a Google Sheet collecting form responses, and a Gmail account.\n\n**How to customize**\nAdd a stage by adding an output to **`route by candidate stage`** and a template page in Notion.\nThe email body is assembled in **`build the email from the template`**.",
        "height": 1148,
        "width": 660,
        "color": 4
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        -1788
      ],
      "id": "sticky-00",
      "name": "Template overview"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 1. Collect the applications\n\nApplications arrive through a Google Form, which writes them to a sheet. **`[cred] Sheets - read new applicants`** reads that sheet on a schedule, and **`Run it now`** does the same by hand.\n\n**`[cred] Notion - read existing candidates`** reads what is already in the database, which is what the next step compares against.",
        "height": 246,
        "width": 760,
        "color": 7
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        -286
      ],
      "id": "sticky-01",
      "name": "1. Collect applications"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 2. The step that stops duplicates\n\n**`drop candidates already in Notion`** compares the sheet against the database and keeps only people who are not there yet.\n\nThis matters more than it looks: a form sheet is append-only and the schedule re-reads it every run, so without this step every applicant would be re-created on every pass. **`new candidate?`** is the gate in front of the write.",
        "height": 312,
        "width": 480,
        "color": 3
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        840,
        520
      ],
      "id": "sticky-02",
      "name": "2. The de-duplication step"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 3. Add, then mark the row processed\n\nThe candidate is created in Notion first, and only then is the sheet row marked.\n\nThat order is deliberate: if the Notion write fails, the row stays unprocessed and the next run retries it. Marking first would lose the applicant silently.",
        "height": 224,
        "width": 760,
        "color": 7
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1400,
        -264
      ],
      "id": "sticky-03",
      "name": "3. Add and mark processed"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 4. Why the previous stage is stored\n\nNotion's trigger says *a page changed* — not *what changed about it*. On its own that is not enough to decide which email to send.\n\n**`read the previous stage`** and **`store the new stage`** keep the last known stage in a data table, so the workflow can compare and act on the actual transition. Without this, a candidate could be emailed again every time anyone edited their page.",
        "height": 246,
        "width": 1040,
        "color": 6
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        -586
      ],
      "id": "sticky-04",
      "name": "4. Why the previous stage is stored"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 5. One template per stage\n\n**`route by candidate stage`** sends each transition down its own branch, and the matching template is fetched from Notion rather than hard-coded here.\n\nThat means changing the wording of a rejection or an interview invitation is a Notion edit by whoever owns the hiring process — not a workflow edit.",
        "height": 224,
        "width": 760,
        "color": 7
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1120,
        880
      ],
      "id": "sticky-05",
      "name": "5. One template per stage"
    }
  ],
